/**
@mainpage IPPL cosmology: model, algorithms and validation

This manual documents the implementation in `demos/cosmology` and its external
validation harnesses. It distinguishes the governing equations, discrete
operators, file contracts and the evidence supporting individual claims.
The source tree is the authority for implemented behavior; papers supply
scientific context, not a claim that every method in those papers is implemented.

@section doc_navigation Reading the manual

1. @ref cosmology_model "Model, units and linear growth": physical definitions
   and the exact potential/momentum convention.
2. @ref cosmology_numerics "Discrete algorithms": Fourier signs, Gaussian
   initialization, CIC, Nyquist handling and kick-drift-kick integration.
3. @ref cosmology_spectra "Independent observables": direct Fourier sums,
   assignment windows, interlacing, shell power and shot noise.
4. @ref cosmology_contracts "Configuration and file contracts": accepted
   parameters, snapshots, diagnostic imports and failure behavior.
5. @ref cosmology_parallel "Parallel execution": collectives, ownership,
   memory placement, host copies and reproducibility.
6. @ref cosmology_validation "Validation and limits": which checks establish
   which property, without promoting a passed integrity audit to physics accuracy.
7. @ref cosmology_tools "Tool and workflow catalog": every maintained source
   file, its purpose, invocation and relation to the API.
8. @ref cosmology_references "References and implementation provenance".

@section doc_core Core API

The model is expressed by @ref cosmology::Config, @ref cosmology::Background,
@ref cosmology::PowerSpectrum, @ref cosmology::Particles and
@ref cosmology::Simulation. The normal application generates its own initial
conditions. `CompareCosmologyForce` and `CompareCosmologyEvolution` are diagnostic
imports into the same production force/integration routines, not independent
IPPL implementations.

@section doc_scope Supported scope

The physical model is periodic, flat, radiation-free Lambda-CDM with one
collisionless matter species and Gaussian first-order Lagrangian initial data
(or a uniform lattice or prescribed sine mode). There is no hydrodynamics,
neutrino species, primordial non-Gaussianity, 2LPT, adaptive force mesh,
short-range correction or production restart interface in the IPPL application.
The stored force is a scaled comoving force, and the stored momentum is not a
velocity in km/s. The local qualification concerns OpenMP and tested MPI ranks;
GPU/multi-node/performance claims require separate evidence.

@section doc_build Build and check the documentation

From the repository root:
@code{.sh}
/Users/adelmann/.venv-h6/bin/python -B demos/cosmology/python/build_documentation.py
cmake --build build_openmp --target cosmology_docs
@endcode

The builder requires Doxygen and BibTeX. It writes HTML, XML, a warning log and
a machine-readable coverage report under the selected build directory; it
changes no simulation outputs. Mathematical formulas use offline SVG when the complete TeX/Ghostscript/PDF-to-SVG toolchain is present,
with an explicit MathJax alternative on machines without those tools. The strict
build fails on documentation errors and on missing required API contracts.
See @ref cosmology_documentation_quality for the checks and their limits.

@defgroup cosmology_core Core physical and numerical API
@brief Configuration, background, initial spectrum, particle state and evolution.
@defgroup cosmology_diagnostics Diagnostic adapters and sanity tests
@brief Manufactured operators, migrated attributes and shared-CSV imports.
@defgroup cosmology_reference External reference harnesses
@brief Native FastPM, original Zarija and pinned GADGET-2 build contracts.
@defgroup cosmology_python Python validation and analysis
@brief Host-side fixtures, observables, comparisons, archives and figures.
*/
